--TEST--
an accept with nobody connecting throws a `TimeoutError` at its bound, a bound that is not positive is refused before anything waits, and the same listener still takes the next connection — `rule:core-classes/net-one-api-three-transports` and `rule:core-api/failure-throws`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Net\Listener::accept
// Nobody has connected, so the wait runs out. A `TimeoutError` and not an
// `IOError`: nothing failed, and the caller's question is whether to wait longer.
var $door = Core\Net::listen("127.0.0.1", 0);
try {
    $door->accept(20ms);
    echo "accepted nobody", "\n";
} catch (TimeoutError $late) {
    echo "late: ", $late->message, "\n";
}

// A bound of zero waits for nothing, so it is refused before the socket is asked.
try {
    $door->accept(0ns);
    echo "accepted within nothing", "\n";
} catch (TimeoutError $late) {
    echo "late: ", $late->message, "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $wrong) {
    echo "wrong: ", $wrong->message, "\n";
}

// The deadline belongs to the one call that set it. A listener that ran out of
// time twice still takes the connection that is waiting, and the stream it
// returns is the server's end of the client's connection.
var $client = Core\Net::connect("127.0.0.1", $door->port(), 5s);
var $served = $door->accept(5s);
$client->write("ping" as bytes, 5s);
echo "heard: ", $served->read(64, 5s) as string, "\n";

// A third accept with nobody left in the backlog runs out of time again: one
// connect is one accepted connection, never two.
try {
    $door->accept(20ms);
    echo "accepted twice", "\n";
} catch (TimeoutError $late) {
    echo "late again", "\n";
}

$served->close();
$client->close();
$door->close();
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.net]
listen = true
connect = ["127.0.0.1"]
internal = ["127.0.0.1"]
--EXPECT--
late: Core\Net\Listener::accept ran out of time waiting on the socket
wrong: Core\Net\Listener::accept: a bound must be a positive length of time
heard: ping
late again
